<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hattusili III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1267–1237 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hattusili III was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> in the thirteenth century BCE. He concluded a peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, one of the earliest known international peace treaties. The treaty is a key part of the Hittite legacy in diplomacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii.jpg" alt="Hattusili III" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krähenstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felsrelief_von_Firaktin_Hattusili_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Much of what is known of Hattusili's childhood comes from the Apology, a biographical account written on a stone tablet during his reign. He was the youngest of four children of the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a> and Queen Gassulawiya. By his own account he was a sickly child who was not expected to reach adulthood, and he credited the goddess Ishtar with saving his life and remained her devoted patron. As the youngest son he did not inherit the throne, which passed to his older brother Muwattalli II.</p><p>Before becoming king he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puduhepa">Puduhepa</a>, a priestess of Ishtar, who later became an important queen in her own right. They had three children, among them his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under Muwattalli II he governed the northern lands of the empire, put down opposition by force and turned to conquering new territory around them. When the king moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> to Tarhuntassa, Hattusili was left to suppress the rebellions that followed. On Muwattalli's death his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhi-teshub">Urhi-Teshub</a>, the son of a concubine, became king as Mursili III. Hattusili at first supported him, since Muwattalli had wished it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhi-teshub">Mursili III</a> soon moved the capital back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, which reduced Hattusili's power, and stripped him of all his territories except Hapkis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerik">Nerik</a>. When Nerik was taken from him too, Hattusili set out to seize the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After deposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urhi-teshub">Mursili III</a>, Hattusili exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a>, another son of Muwattalli II whom he had raised himself, to govern Tarhuntassa. He kept up correspondence with many neighbouring kingdoms, and his letters with the Egyptian pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> led, roughly fifteen years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, to the first recorded peace treaty, the Eternal Treaty, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kadesh">Treaty of Kadesh</a>.</p><p>His reign is notable for the large body of letters and accounts found from it: more than two hundred letters were unearthed at the royal palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>. They include the Apology, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawagalawa-letter">Tawagalawa letter</a> and the Arzawa letters, and are among the few primary sources for the Hittite empire of the time. His eldest son Nerikkaili was heir apparent, but Hattusili removed him from the succession in favour of the younger Tudhaliya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ḫattušili III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The peace treaty of 1258 BCE, concluded with Muwatalli's successor <b>Hattusili III</b> and the earliest such treaty to survive in both parties' versions, in effect acknowledged a strategic stalemate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Kadesh, concluded between <b>Hattusili III</b> and Ramesses II around 1259 BCE, is the earliest peace treaty to survive in the versions of both parties: in Akkadian cuneiform at Hattusa and in Egyptian hieroglyphs at Karnak and the Ramesseum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Tawagalawa letter, probably written by <b>Hattusili III</b>, the Hittite king addresses the king of Ahhiyawa as his “brother”, complains of the raider Piyamaradu, who operated from Millawanda (Miletus), and recalls an earlier quarrel over Wilusa, usually identified with Troy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaska raiders sacked Hattusa in the 14th century BCE, and <b>Hattusili III</b> only recovered Nerik in the 13th.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaska">Kaska</a></li><li><blockquote>Bentesina was deposed for siding with Egypt after Kadesh but restored by <b>Hattusili III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-amurru">Kingdom of Amurru</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tawagalawa letter of <b>Hattusili III</b>, c. 1250 BCE, names Atpa as the local ruler, son-in-law of the rebel Piyamaradu, who used Millawanda as a base for raids on Hittite lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/millawanda">Millawanda</a></li></ul></section>
